56:
"'At the foot of Chātaka Mountain, there were three bamboo poles;
The Latāyaṭṭhi was white like a silver pole and resembled the color of gold.
On the Pupphayaṭṭhi, there were flowers of blue and other colors as they exist (in the world);
On the Sakuṇayaṭṭhi, birds were situated in their natural forms.' (So it is said in the Dīpavaṃsa).

Due to the power of King Devānampiyatissa's merit, many kinds of treasures such as pearls, gems, and lapis lazuli also arose from the ocean. In the island of Tambapaṇṇidīpa, eight types of pearls appeared: horse-pearl, elephant-pearl, chariot-pearl, myrobalan-pearl, bracelet-pearl, ring-pearl, kakudha-fruit-pearl, and common pearl. That King sent those bamboo poles, those pearls, and many other treasures as gifts to King Asoka the Righteous.

King Asoka, being pleased, sent to King Devānampiyatissa the five royal regalia: the white parasol, the yak-tail whisk, the sword, the turban, and the jeweled sandals, as well as many other gifts for the purpose of consecration. Those gifts were: a conch, water from the Ganges (water brought from Lake Anotatta), face powder, a head ornament, a golden pitcher, a Nandiyāvaṭṭa vessel, a palanquin, a maiden, a ladle, a pair of unwashed cloths, a hand-towel, sandalwood, dawn-colored clay, eye-ointment, gallnut, and myrobalan. This is also stated in the Dīpavaṃsa:

'The yak-tail whisk, the turban, the white parasol, the sword, and the sandals;
The head-wrap, the noble neck-ornament, the golden pitcher, the Nandivaṭṭaka vessel;
The palanquin, the conch, the head ornament, and the pair of unwashed cloths;
The golden bowl, the ladle, the costly hand-towel;
Water from Lake Anotatta, the maiden, the excellent sandalwood;
The dawn-colored clay, the eye-ointment brought by the Nāgas;
The gallnut, the myrobalan, the costly deathless medicine;
Six thousand wagon-loads of fragrant hill-paddy brought by parrots,
Produced by the power of merit—these did the King named Asoka send.'

Not only did he send these material gifts, but it is said he also sent this gift of Dhamma:
'I have gone for refuge to the Buddha, the Dhamma, and the Saṅgha;
I have declared my status as a lay follower (upāsaka) in the Dispensation of the Son of the Sakyans.'

78:
(Dispassion is) for the sake of liberation (vimutti), which is the fruit of Arahantship. Liberation is for the sake of the knowledge and vision of liberation, which is the knowledge of reviewing (paccavekkhaṇa-ñāṇa) that perceives the fruit of Arahantship. The knowledge and vision of liberation is for the sake of the cessation of defilements without clinging to any object through the four types of grasping (anupādā-parinibbāna). Discussion of the Vinaya, consultation on the Vinaya, the causal sequence, and listening—all these have this anupādā-parinibbāna as their benefit. That liberation of the mind from defilements without clinging also has this anupādā-parinibbāna as its benefit (Pari. 366). Since there are such great benefits, one should apply oneself diligently to the study of the Vinaya.

By this much sequence of words, for the sake of well-explaining the Vinaya, the following summary (mātikā) was established:

"By whom it was spoken, when, and for what reason;
By whom it was upheld, and by whom it was brought;
And where this [Vinaya] is established;
Having stated this sequence, thereafter [I shall explain the Vinaya]."

The meaning of this verse has been clearly shown first. The explanation of the External Nidāna (Bahira-nidāna) of the Vinaya has also been explained according to what was intended.

## The Discussion on the Third Council is finished.
## The Discussion on the External Nidāna is finished.
